<article class="env-remark" id="F4BB60">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/F4BB60">Remark <span data-tag="F4BB60">4.2.2.6</span>.</a> The hypothesis on $s$ in Proposition <a href="/tag/BFE09D" data-tag="BFE09D">4.2.2.4</a> can be weakened: all we need in the proof is existence of maps $M^{op}(\phi ) \rightarrow K \times _{S} \Delta ^ n$ which are universal categorical equivalences (that is, induce categorical equivalences $M^{op}(\phi ) \times _{ \Delta ^ n } T \rightarrow K \times _{S} T$ for any $T \rightarrow \Delta ^ n$). Consequently, Proposition <a href="/tag/BFE09D" data-tag="BFE09D">4.2.2.4</a> remains valid when $K \simeq S \times K^0$, for <em>any</em> simplicial set $K^0$ (not necessarily an $\infty $-category). It seems likely that Proposition <a href="/tag/BFE09D" data-tag="BFE09D">4.2.2.4</a> remains valid whenever $s$ is a smooth map of simplicial sets, but we have not been able to prove this. </p>
</article>